@page "/guides/analyzers"

<PageTitle>Analyzers · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<DocsArticle Class="docs-article--guide">
    <Contents>
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="rules" Text="Rules" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="what-it-can-see" Text="What it can see" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="turning-a-rule-off" Text="Turning a rule off" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="related" Text="Related" />
    </Contents>
    <Header>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Analyzers</TbText>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
            The Trailblazor package includes an analyzer that reads your markup as the app builds. It warns, at the line
            in the <code>.razor</code> file, about a control or field screen readers can't name, a dialog with no title,
            and a value that snaps back after the user changes it.
        </TbText>
    </Header>
    <ChildContent>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section" id="rules">Rules</TbText>
        <TbTable aria-label="Analyzer rules">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Rule</th>
                    <th scope="col">Warns when</th>
                    <th scope="col">Fix</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">TB001</th>
                    <td>
                        A control that needs a name has none, or a blank one: an icon button, menu, popover, toolbar,
                        toolbar button, chip group, toggle group, progress, tab, tree, submenu, option group, sortable
                        list or insert divider.
                    </td>
                    <td>Set the parameter the warning names, such as <code>AriaLabel</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">TB002</th>
                    <td>
                        A <code>TbDialog</code>'s <code>Title</code>, or the title passed to
                        <code>IDialogService</code>, is blank.
                    </td>
                    <td>Give the dialog a title. It names the dialog and is announced as the dialog opens.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">TB003</th>
                    <td>
                        An input is given a <code>Value</code> without <code>ValueChanged</code>, so the page's next
                        render undoes the user's change.
                    </td>
                    <td>
                        Use <code>@@bind-Value</code> or handle <code>ValueChanged</code>. A field can set
                        <code>ReadOnly</code> instead.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">TB004</th>
                    <td>A field has no <code>Label</code> and no <code>AriaLabel</code>.</td>
                    <td>Set <code>Label</code>, or <code>AriaLabel</code> when the layout has no room for one.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </TbTable>
        <CodeBlock Label="Search.razor" Code="@Mistakes" />
        <CodeBlock Label="The build's warnings" Code="@Warnings" Language="CodeLanguage.Plain" />

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section" id="what-it-can-see">
            What it can see
        </TbText>
        <TbText>
            The analyzer reads the values written in markup. A name held in a variable or worked out by an expression
            isn't known until the page runs, so the analyzer leaves it to the component. Most of the controls above
            check it when they render and throw if it's blank.
        </TbText>

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">What names a field</TbText>
        <TbText>
            An attribute written on most fields goes on the wrapper, not the input, so it doesn't name the field. These
            do:
        </TbText>
        <div class="docs-api" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="What names a field">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Component</th>
                        <th scope="col">Named by</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Any field but a radio or checkbox group</th>
                        <td>An <code>aria-label</code> in <code>InputAttributes</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">A field with a native input, such as a text field or checkbox</th>
                        <td>
                            Also an <code>id</code>, so a <code>TbLabel</code> elsewhere can name it. A select, list box
                            or range can't be named that way.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Radio and checkbox groups</th>
                        <td>
                            <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code> written on the group, since a
                            group's attributes go on its fieldset
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <TbText>
            A tree, which isn't a field, is named by its <code>AriaLabel</code> or by another element through
            <code>aria-labelledby</code>.
        </TbText>

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" As="TextElement.H3">What it leaves alone</TbText>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Attributes spread with <code>@@attributes</code> may hold a name, so a component given them isn't
                checked.
            </li>
            <li>
                A required parameter that's left out, such as an icon button's <code>AriaLabel</code>, already gets
                Razor's own RZ2012 warning, so TB001 warns only when it is blank.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section" id="turning-a-rule-off">
            Turning a rule off
        </TbText>
        <TbText>
            The rules are warnings, or errors in a project that treats warnings as errors. To turn one off for a whole
            project, list it in <code>NoWarn</code>.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="MyApp.csproj" Code="@NoWarn" />

        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section" id="related">Related</TbText>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="guides/testing">Testing</a>: find components by the names these rules ask for.</li>
            <li><a href="components/label">Label</a>: name a field from elsewhere on the page.</li>
        </ul>
    </ChildContent>
</DocsArticle>

@code {
    private const string Mistakes = """
        <TbIconButton Icon="Icons.Search" AriaLabel="" />
        <TbTextField @bind-Value="_query" />
        <TbCheckbox Label="Only open trails" Value="true" />
        """;

    private const string Warnings = """
        Search.razor(1,35): warning TB001: TbIconButton has no accessible name.
            Set AriaLabel.
        Search.razor(2,20): warning TB004: TbTextField has no label. Set Label, or
            AriaLabel when the layout has no room for one.
        Search.razor(3,38): warning TB003: TbCheckbox sets Value without ValueChanged,
            so the parent's next render undoes the user's change. Use @bind-Value,
            handle ValueChanged, or set ReadOnly.
        """;

    private const string NoWarn = """
        <PropertyGroup>
          <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);TB003</NoWarn>
        </PropertyGroup>
        """;
}
